/T (1. Having an appreciation for the workings of another person's mind is considered a prerequisite for natural language acquisition, strategic social interaction, reflexive thought, and moral judgment. 2. It is a 'theory of mind' though some scholars prefer to call it 'mentalizing' or 'mindreading', which is important for the development of one's cognitive abilities. 3. Though we must speculate about its evolutionary origin, we do have indications that the capacity evolved sometime in the last few million years. 4. This capacity develops from early beginnings in the first year of life to the adult's fast and often effortless understanding of others' thoughts, feelings, and intentions. 5. One of the most fascinating human capacities is the ability to perceive and interpret other people's behaviour in terms of their mental states.)
/E (1. Having an appreciation for the workings of another person's mind is considered a prerequisite for natural language acquisition, strategic social interaction, reflexive thought, and moral judgment. 2. It is a 'theory of mind' though some scholars prefer to call it 'mentalizing' or 'mindreading', which is important for the development of one's cognitive abilities. 3. Though we must speculate about its evolutionary origin, we do have indications that the capacity evolved sometime in the last few million years. 4. This capacity develops from early beginnings in the first year of life to the adult's fast and often effortless understanding of others' thoughts, feelings, and intentions. 5. One of the most fascinating human capacities is the ability to perceive and interpret other people's behaviour in terms of their mental states.)
